<!-- khtpm_strip_header.chtpm — layout for the persistent top-left header
     window (hq_win, the 14-cell HQ/USER/file/desks/pals/palettes/edit/
     player/db/plugins/menus/store/network/date-time strip) AND its submenu popup content
     (popup_win), both rendered/hit-tested by khtpm_strip_parser.c against
     this ONE tag tree.

     One-file-vs-two-file decision (khtpm-strip-parser-SCOPE.md's open
     call, "lean toward two [...] matching the two-window reality"):
     RESOLVED AS TWO FILES. This file owns the header window's cell row and
     its submenu content; khtpm_strip_bottom.chtpm (sibling file, same dir)
     owns the separate bottom tab-bar window. Matches the two persistent
     X11 windows khtpm_strip_parser.c already creates (hq_win + win), one
     layout file per window rather than one file describing both — a
     single <panel> per window keeps each file's structure legible on its
     own, and the two windows have genuinely different geometry/render
     shapes (horizontal cell row vs. vertical tab/shortcut stack) that
     don't share much by being combined.

     Tag vocabulary: <panel> <text> <button label=".." onClick="..">
     <row> <cli_io target_id=".." label=".."> — the locked 5-tag set from
     SCOPE.md, nothing else.

     STRUCTURE NOTE (found only once actually implementing against
     chtpm_parser.c's real is_navigable()/is_descendant(), not assumed from
     the scope doc's summary of them): an ACTIVATE button's submenu content
     must be its own TAG-TREE CHILD (nested inside a non-self-closing
     <button>...</button>), not a sibling element after a self-closing
     <button/>. is_descendant() walks parent_index chains, so a sibling
     row would never resolve as a descendant of the button that opened it,
     breaking the whole ACTIVATE-scope mechanism. This matches real CHTPM's
     own usage exactly (an ACTIVATE button wraps its own child rows) —
     the scope doc's illustrative example (a wrapping <row> containing a
     sibling <button onClick="ACTIVATE"/> plus a sibling ${var} row) was
     "illustrative, not final" per its own text, and this is the concrete
     correction made once implementing against the real reference.

     Cell order matches khtpm_taskbar_manager.h's KtbState comment and
     tp_taskbar.c's load_strip_config()+cells[] assembly EXACTLY: 0=HQ,
     1=USER, 2=file, 3=desks, 4=pals, 5=palettes, 6=edit, 7=player, 8=db,
     9=plugins, 10=menus, 11=store, 12=network, 13=ai, 14=date-time. onClick="ACTIVATE:<n>"
     (n = cell index+1, matching khtpm_strip_codes.h's KSC_HQ_HEADER_BASE+n) is a
     necessary, documented extension of real CHTPM's bare "ACTIVATE" onClick
     value: this strip's submenu CONTENT is manager-authored per-cell (the
     manager only knows which cell's rows to publish into ${strip_hq_items}
     once it's told "which" cell was opened), unlike real CHTPM where an
     ACTIVATE menu's full child tree already lives in the static layout
     file. The parser still treats ACTIVATE as a 100% local scope toggle
     (lay_activate()/lay_is_activate_marker() match on the "ACTIVATE"
     PREFIX) — the ":<n>" suffix is ADDITIONALLY forwarded to the manager
     as one extra code so it knows what to populate, not a departure from
     the local-scope mechanism itself. USER (cell 1) WAS NOT an ACTIVATE
     button as of the legacy port (tp_taskbar.c/ktb_strip_user_activate()
     treated it as a direct action with no submenu, onClick "STRIP:2") -
     changed 2026-08-11 (direct instruction: "there is no way to create new
     user in 2. user tab yet ... going forward i want to use only livedesk
     as the interface, unique user accounts") to a real ACTIVATE:2 button
     with a manager-built submenu (New User / Switch: rows / Logout, see
     livedesk_build_user_menu() in khtpm_taskbar_manager.c). See
     au11-hq/USER_CREATION.md for the full design writeup, including why
     the new-user signup is a sequential 2-step cli_io flow rather than
     tpmos's simultaneous multi-field form (khtpm's cli_io is a single leaf
     per screen, not a multi-widget layout).

     ${strip_hq_items} is ONE manager-published var shared by every cell
     that can show a submenu (HQ/file/desks/pals/player — the same ones
     khtpm_taskbar_manager.h's own KtbState comment names as the only cells
     that ever populate hq_menu[]), since the manager only ever has ONE
     open popup's rows in flight at a time (hq_menu[] is reused across
     cells, not per-cell). Only the currently-ACTIVATEd cell's own children
     are ever actually navigable/rendered (is_navigable()'s ACTIVATE-scope
     check hides every other cell's identical-content children), so the
     repetition below is harmless — the manager guarantees ${strip_hq_items}
     matches whichever cell is currently open.

     <cli_io> is a special-cased leaf (SCOPE.md's own words), not nested
     under any cell's scope — it is driven entirely by the manager's
     cliio_active/cliio_typing/cliio_buffer/cliio_op state (opened as a
     side effect of activating an hq-item row like "new session"/"rename
     desk", independent of which header cell's ACTIVATE scope is/was open)
     and is only ever made locally navigable by khtpm_strip_parser.c
     explicitly lay_activate()-ing it when it sees cliio_active=="1" — see
     khtpm_strip_layout.c's lay_is_navigable() for the structural rule that
     makes this safe (a cli_io element is never navigable except when it
     IS the active_index). -->
<panel>
  <button label="HQ" onClick="ACTIVATE:1">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="${username}" onClick="ACTIVATE:2" sprite="${avatar_dir}">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="${file_label}" onClick="ACTIVATE:3">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="${desks_label}" onClick="ACTIVATE:4">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="pals" onClick="ACTIVATE:5">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="palettes" onClick="ACTIVATE:6">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="edit" onClick="ACTIVATE:7"/>
  <button label="player" onClick="ACTIVATE:8">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="db" onClick="ACTIVATE:9">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="plugins" onClick="ACTIVATE:10"/>
  <button id="toys" label="toys" onClick="ACTIVATE:11">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="store" onClick="ACTIVATE:12"/>
  <button label="network" onClick="ACTIVATE:13"/>
  <button label="h-ai" onClick="ACTIVATE:14">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <button label="${datetime}" onClick="ACTIVATE:15">
    <row>${strip_hq_items}</row>
  </button>
  <!-- REAL, NEW 2026-08-16, direct request - real, static, per-launch
       identity marker (PID as keycap-digit unicode + a clock-face
       emoji for launch time, NOT live-ticking, NOT an ACTIVATE cell -
       just a display-only <text>, matching the user's own wording
       "final field", after 15/date-time, not part of the numbered
       cells). See khtpm_strip_parser.c's own build_uid_init(). -->
  <text label="${build_uid}"/>
  <cli_io target_id="cliio" label="${cliio_label}"/>
</panel>
